work.prompt:
Question: ${question}
Atomic task: ${item}
Read only. Open sources rather than trusting snippets, prior context or other agents. Return individual one-fact claims with short verbatim excerpt, reference (local path/line or opened URL), source identity including the publication date the source shows, or 'undated' (never supply a date yourself), and sourceKind: primary when the source originated the fact or is its authoritative record (official docs or spec, source code, the author's own announcement, repository or paper, a standards body or registry); secondary for reporting or commentary about it (news, blogs, social posts, aggregators, wikis, third-party vendor pages). Do not invent citations. If inaccessible or contested, report a gap; distinguish absence of evidence from disproof. Return only structured JSON {claims:[{claim,excerpt,reference,source,sourceKind}],gaps:[string]}.

evaluator.prompt:
Judge research coverage from supplied evidence only; you have no tools. Original question: ${question}
Plan: ${plan}
Evidence ledger (claim ids r<round>-<item>-<claim>, round 0 = seed; duplicates merged; tasks already run; failures; opened references; openGaps from your previous decision; exhaustedGaps already had two tasks with no closing claim): ${feedback}
For each plan part decide supported (opened-source claims answer it directly), partial, or missing; prioritize the central product/scope question over citation polish or commit pins. Every plan part is material; never call a plan part immaterial. A part supported only by secondary claims is partial; create a task for a primary source. Carry every worker-reported gap forward and list it until a later claim closes it; name gaps <partId>-<slug>. Worker excerpts, snippets and schema validity do not prove facts; do not count two views of one source as independent; absence of evidence is not disproof. For a material gap, create one task with a distinct access route not yet tried: another primary source, the site's public API or mirror, an archive copy, an omitted upstream README or API/CLI source, or backward chaining when an opened source points to earlier mentions (a cited post, a linked paper, 'surfaced this weekend'). To check a disputed, consequential or weak claim, create a task that reopens its reference and quotes the passage. For UI existence, task routes, assets or official product boundaries. Never repeat a task listed in the ledger's tasks. Never create a task for a gap in exhaustedGaps; it already had two tasks without a closing claim. Keep it in gaps as inaccessible. A gap is inaccessible only after a distinct route failed. Decision rule: return decision:'continue' with linked tasks when any part is not supported and a gap has an untried accessible route; otherwise return decision:'sufficient', tasks:[], listing every remaining open or inaccessible gap. Return gaps:[] only when every part is supported and no worker gap remains open. Never stop because a round or task bound is reached; the runtime handles bounds. Return only {decision:'sufficient'|'continue',gaps:[{id,description}],tasks:[{gapId,item:{source,question,purpose,partIds}}]}.

synthesize.prompt:
Produce a cited Markdown research report and structured findings. Question: ${question}
Context (unverified leads): ${context}
Plan parts: ${plan}
Autonomous scope check approved the plan: ${scopeApproved}
Research ledger (claims with ids r<round>-<item>-<claim>, gaps, failures, opened references) and terminal research status: ${research}
You have no tools. Use only validated structured claims with opened source excerpts/provenance for factual claims; worker excerpts and search snippets are not independently verified unless a later worker task reopened the source. A part whose cited claims are all secondary is partial, not supported; never mark a secondary-only finding independently-checked. Copy each reference exactly as the ledger shows it, character for character; never rewrite its host (for example api.fxtwitter.com to x.com), path (for example GitHub blob/ to raw) or query. Cite only references that appear in a worker claim; never add a source no worker claim references. If the scope check did not approve the plan, say so in the report and treat affected parts cautiously. Take dates only from a claim's source or excerpt; never state access dates. Report every dated claim relevant to a plan part, or list it in rejectedClaims with a reason. Mark single-source, disputed and independently checked findings separately (verification: independently-checked, single-source, or disputed). Independent checks are agent judgment, not a schema truth proof. Cite each factual sentence with ledger claim ids in brackets, for example [r1-2-1]; end the Markdown with a Sources list that gives each cited id, its source identity and its reference; put the same ids in claimIds of accepted findings and coverage rows. Never cite an id or URL the ledger does not contain. Include limitations. Reject unsupported claims; preserve every failure, unverified gap and contested finding even when bounded feedback says sufficient. Report verifiedCoverage exactly once per plan part id. A material failure, unresolved material gap, or disputed finding downgrades its part to partial or missing; immaterial or recovered failures and gaps stay reported without downgrading. Outcome follows coverage: succeeded when every part is supported (omit reason); partial when useful findings remain but a part is partial or missing (reason names unresolved parts); failed when no reliable findings remain. Return structured JSON only; never write an output file (runtime handles oversized result artifacts).
